course: dictionary
lesson: 4671
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one the writer means. The word holding is a good example. It is the -ing form of the verb hold, so it can mean having something in your hands, or keeping it inside. «A chip is a small piece of material holding millions or billions of transistors.» Here holding means containing.
explain: Holding can also be a noun. A holding is land or property that someone owns, like a small farm. Holdings can be things like money, shares or books. Look at the clues: a farm, a bank or a sports game each points to a different meaning.
explain: Holding is also a sport word. In football or netball, holding is an unfair move where you grab another player. Ask yourself what fits: holding a cup, holding some land, or holding someone back? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «Breath-holding contests are dangerous, so this is a bet to walk away from.» Here holding means keeping your breath in for as long as you can. Same word, different meaning: the context always decides.
fact: Holding is the -ing form of hold: hold, holds, held, holding.
fact: A holding is land or property that someone owns, like a small farm.
fact: Holdings are things like money, shares or books that a person or group owns or keeps.
fact: In sport, holding is an unfair move where you grab another player.
fact: Useful phrases: holding hands, holding on, holding still, holding area.
exercise: «The jug is holding 2 litres of water.» What does holding mean here? | Owning a farm | Grabbing another player | *Containing, or keeping inside | Dropping it on the floor
exercise: «Her family owns a small holding in the hills.» What is a holding here? | A rough hug | Something you hold in your hands | A rule in a game | *Land or property that someone owns
exercise: In football, a player is given a penalty for holding. What is holding? | Scoring a goal | Kicking the ball very high | Running fast with the ball | *Grabbing another player unfairly
test: «The little ones were holding hands as they crossed the road.» What does holding mean here? | Owning the road | Stopping a car | *Gripping each other's hands | Keeping their breath in
test: «The bank reported its holdings of shares and money.» What are holdings here? | A sports penalty | Places where people wait | *Things like money and shares that a group owns | Hands held up in the air
exam: «Keep holding the rail as you go down the stairs.» What does holding mean here? | *Keeping your hand gripped on something | Throwing the rail away | Stopping a race | Owning the stairs
